{"name":"True Romance","hex":"#4d456a","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"true","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Conforming to the actual state of reality or fact; factually correct.","As an ellipsis of \"(while) it is true (that)\", used to start a sentence"]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Accurately; in alignment.","Truthfully."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/true"},{"term":"romance","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An intimate relationship between two people; a love affair.","A strong obsession or attachment for something or someone."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To woo; to court.","To write or tell romantic stories, poetry, letters, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/romance"}],"entries":[{"title":"True Romance","blocks":[{"text":"True Romance is a 1993 American romantic crime drama film directed by Tony Scott and written by Quentin Tarantino. It stars Christian Slater and Patricia Arquette, with Dennis Hopper, Val Kilmer, Gary Oldman, Brad Pitt, and Christopher Walken in supporting roles. Slater and Arquette portray newlyweds who head to California with a stolen suitcase of drugs, with the Mafia in close pursuit and their future very uncertain."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/d/d6/True_romance.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/True_Romance"},{"title":"True Romance (Charli XCX album)","blocks":[{"text":"True Romance is the debut studio album by British singer Charli XCX, released on 12 April 2013 by Asylum and Atlantic Records. Originally scheduled for release to would be on 12 April 2012, the album's release was delayed for exactly 1 year and had been in the making since 2010 when Charli XCX met with producer Ariel Rechtshaid in Los Angeles. To promote the album's release, Charli XCX embarked on a tour throughout the United Kingdom in April 2013."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/d/d2/Charli_XCX_-_True_Romance.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/True_Romance_(Charli_XCX_album)"},{"title":"Romance","blocks":[{"text":"Romance and romantic love encompass a number of ideas about love, which are interrelated for historical and cultural reasons:"},{"items":["passionate feelings of attraction—a mental state of \"being in love\", with focused attention (salience) towards a specific individual for courtship or pair bonding;","the cultural practice or idealization of initiating intimate relationships for feelings like these, over more practical or ordinary concerns;","a relationship or love affair initiated or maintained this way, which may be premarital or absent a commitment; and","a love story involving these elements."]}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Frank_Bernard_Dicksee_-_Romeo_and_Juliet%2C_1884.jpg/330px-Frank_Bernard_Dicksee_-_Romeo_and_Juliet%2C_1884.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Romance"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Mesocorticolimbic_Circuit.png/500px-Mesocorticolimbic_Circuit.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Mesocorticolimbic_Circuit.png/500px-Mesocorticolimbic_Circuit.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Dopamine is produced in the ventral tegmental area (VTA) of the brain, and projected to the nucleus accumbens (NAc). Dopamine activity in the NAc is key to the attribution of salience.[84]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMesocorticolimbic_Circuit.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Colour_Wheel_of_Love.jpeg/500px-Colour_Wheel_of_Love.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Colour_Wheel_of_Love.jpeg/1280px-Colour_Wheel_of_Love.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Colour_Wheel_of_Love.jpeg/500px-Colour_Wheel_of_Love.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AColour_Wheel_of_Love.jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Crystaled_salt_on_the_stick.jpg/500px-Crystaled_salt_on_the_stick.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Crystaled_salt_on_the_stick.jpg/1280px-Crystaled_salt_on_the_stick.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Crystaled_salt_on_the_stick.jpg/500px-Crystaled_salt_on_the_stick.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"\"Crystallization\" was coined by the 19th-century French writer Stendhal to refer to the tendency of a person in love to overemphasize the positive aspects of their loved one and overlook the negative (\"love is blind\").[121][122][123][5] This idealization is now considered positive illusions, and significant scientific evidence has shown that it contributes to relationship satisfaction, long-term well-being and decreased risk for relationship discontinuation.[122][124]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACrystaled_salt_on_the_stick.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Marianne_Stokes05.jpg/500px-Marianne_Stokes05.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Marianne_Stokes05.jpg/500px-Marianne_Stokes05.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Aucassin and Nicolette, by Marianne Stokes","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMarianne_Stokes05.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/CADAL07013412_%E9%A7%90%E6%98%A5%E5%9C%92_%E7%AC%AC2%E7%89%88.djvu/page1-500px-CADAL07013412_%E9%A7%90%E6%98%A5%E5%9C%92_%E7%AC%AC2%E7%89%88.djvu.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/CADAL07013412_%E9%A7%90%E6%98%A5%E5%9C%92_%E7%AC%AC2%E7%89%88.djvu/page1-1280px-CADAL07013412_%E9%A7%90%E6%98%A5%E5%9C%92_%E7%AC%AC2%E7%89%88.djvu.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/CADAL07013412_%E9%A7%90%E6%98%A5%E5%9C%92_%E7%AC%AC2%E7%89%88.djvu/page1-500px-CADAL07013412_%E9%A7%90%E6%98%A5%E5%9C%92_%E7%AC%AC2%E7%89%88.djvu.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cover of Zhuchun yuan (The Garden of Spring Residence) written by Wuhang Yeke, an 18th-century Chinese caizi jiaren (\"scholar and beauty\") romantic novel, a type of romantic fiction[158][159][160]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACADAL07013412_%E9%A7%90%E6%98%A5%E5%9C%92_%E7%AC%AC2%E7%89%88.djvu"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Inclusion_of_other_in_the_self_scale.png/500px-Inclusion_of_other_in_the_self_scale.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Inclusion_of_other_in_the_self_scale.png/500px-Inclusion_of_other_in_the_self_scale.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Inclusion of the other in the self (IOS) is typically measured with the IOS Scale.[190]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AInclusion_of_other_in_the_self_scale.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Slot_machines.jpg/500px-Slot_machines.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Slot_machines.jpg/1280px-Slot_machines.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Slot_machines.jpg/500px-Slot_machines.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Infatuated love essentially thrives under intermittent reinforcement—also the mechanic a slot machine relies on.[202][203]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASlot_machines.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/THORN-155lb-PS-jointed-joint.jpg/500px-THORN-155lb-PS-jointed-joint.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/THORN-155lb-PS-jointed-joint.jpg/500px-THORN-155lb-PS-jointed-joint.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"\"Why does romantic love leave us bewitched, bothered, and bewildered? Could it be another paradoxical tactic like handcuffing oneself to railroad tracks?\" —Steven Pinker[213]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATHORN-155lb-PS-jointed-joint.jpg"}],"sounds":{"true":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e0/En-uk-true.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/e/e0/En-uk-true.ogg/En-uk-true.ogg.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e7/En-us-true.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.w